WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
 - Hello and welcome to chapter five, loops and iteration.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:07.740
 Now we're going to work on our fourth basic pattern

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.260
 on sequential, conditional, store and reuse,

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.460
 and loops and iteration.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.460
 And this is the one where we teach the computer

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.360
 how to do things a lot.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.420
 We can tell to do something a million times.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:24.180
 And so that's where we get the doggedness of computers

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.860
 or the fact that they're so good at doing work for us

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.160
 because we can set them off to a task

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.360
 and they'll do it until it's done.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:36.440
 So here's a very simple loop, a very simple loop.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:40.180
 Let's put the coffee over here.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.920
 The keyword that we're going to start using is the while loop.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.020
 We're also going to use the for later on.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.460
 And the while loop functions very much like an if statement.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.420
 The while starts it and then this is just like an if statement.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.940
 It's a question that leads to a true or a false answer.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.200
 And then there's a colon and then there's an indented block

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.640
 and then we use the deindent to determine

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.900
 how long the loop is and so this print is deindented

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.280
 so that indicates the end of the loop.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:13.880
 And so at some level, what's going to happen here

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:16.820
 is it's just going to run and if this is true,

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:18.500
 it's going to run this code.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.480
 And if it's false, it's going to skip the code.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.340
 And that way it functions like an if.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.520
 The place that it doesn't function like an if

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.780
 is after it's run the code once,

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.580
 it goes up and then asks the question again.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.720
 And so you can think of it going back up

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.520
 kind of to the top of the while loop

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.280
 and then re-asking the question.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.960
 Like, okay, is this going to run again?

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.680
 And then it's going to do that some number of times

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.000
 and then it's going to finish.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.020
 And so that's the loop, that's the iteration.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.820
 And we're going to make a variable.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.900
 We're going to construct very carefully

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.820
 a variable that we call the iteration variable

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:56.660
 and that's n and it's a variable that's going to change

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.340
 and it's our way of running the loop

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.220
 but not running the loop forever.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.100
 So let's just run this.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.800
 We come in and it's five.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:05.700
 Is n greater than zero?

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:06.540
 Yes it is.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.160
 So we're going to run this code.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.120
 So we're going to run this code.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.100
 We're going to print out five.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.340
 Then we're going to subtract one

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.060
 and then we're going to go back up,

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.180
 go back up and ask the question.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.660
 Is n greater than zero?

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:19.380
 And the answer is, since it's four, the answer is yes.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:20.980
 So then it runs again.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.260
 Then it prints out four, subtracts it again,

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:26.300
 checks, prints three, subtracts it again,

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.500
 prints two, subtracts it again,

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.620
 prints one, subtracts it again.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.800
 Now n is zero and so it comes back up,

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.020
 comes back up.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.140
 Is this question has now become false?

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.620
 So it's going to take the exit.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.020
 So it's going to come down and run this line right here.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.140
 Then it prints blast off and we can kind of print out

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:47.340
 the residual value of n just to sort of prove to ourselves

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.900
 that it ran until n was no longer greater than zero

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.260
 and then zero was the final value for n.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:57.420
 We carefully constructed this n, n equals, oops, go back.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.860
 We carefully constructed n, we set it to five,

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:04.100
 then we carefully subtracted one each time through the loop

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.540
 and then we're using that to control when to exit the loop.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.760
 And so you could think of this loop as, for now,

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.980
 running five times, true, true, true, true, true,

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.620
 and then false finally.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.260
 So this question was true for a while

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:20.420
 and as long as it was true, the loop ran

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.740
 and then when it turned false,

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.160
 the loop stopped.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:28.460
 And so this variable that we construct to control the loop

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.980
 was called the iteration variable because it tells

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:34.300
 how many times this loop is going to run over and over

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.800
 or otherwise known as iterate.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.260
 So this is a badly constructed loop

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.660
 with an iteration variable that we didn't do very well.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.500
 And so if we take a look at this,

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.540
 we start out with n five and then this is greater than zero

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.500
 so it's true so it runs it and then it runs it again

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.620
 and then it's still greater than zero.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.060
 So you can pretty much see because we're not changing n,

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.100
 this is gonna be true, true, true, true, dot dot dot dot,

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.380
 forever, true forever.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.620
 And so this is an infinite loop

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:06.020
 and it's just gonna run until your computer runs out

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:08.420
 of battery or you hit the button.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:10.100
 This is the kind of thing where you often see

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.300
 your computer spinning like a spinning beach ball

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:17.300
 or some other indication that your computer's super busy.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:19.180
 It's in some kind of a loop really tight

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:21.220
 and it's running something and it's using up

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.860
 all of the processing resources of your computer.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:25.900
 That's an infinite loop.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.900
 And so the problem is is we did nothing

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.320
 with the iteration variable.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.500
 Now here's a different loop.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:36.100
 And so this one demonstrates a different idea.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.020
 So in this case, we start out with n is zero

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:41.580
 and it comes in here and is n greater than zero?

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.740
 Question mark and the answer is false.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:44.820
 So it skips it.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.700
 It doesn't run these lines of code at all.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.620
 And so this loop doesn't run at all

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.300
 'cause it comes in, asks the question,

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.980
 it says no, and then it skips right around it.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:55.860
 So never run, never run.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:59.340
 And so this actually is, sometimes you write a while loop

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.620
 on purpose like this, not quite as simple as this one,

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:06.240
 but the idea is this emphasizes that these loops

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.880
 are what we call zero trip.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:13.140
 They are not even guaranteed to run once.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:14.900
 They're gonna run maybe zero times.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:17.180
 And in this respect, it functions exactly

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:19.540
 like an if statement, right?

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:20.900
 Meaning the first time through the loop,

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.700
 if it's not true, it's just gonna skip right by it.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:29.300
 So there's a couple of ways of getting out of loops.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.540
 In this case, I'm constructing an infinite loop

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:35.300
 because remember the kind of definition of an infinite loop

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:38.180
 is if this is gonna stay true.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:41.000
 Well true is the constant true.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.700
 So this is gonna run forever.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:44.740
 And what it's gonna do is it's gonna prompt

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:49.020
 with a little arrow and then let us type

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:52.420
 and read whatever we type into the variable line.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.620
 And then if the line is done, we're gonna break.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.180
 Now break is an executable statement.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:01.940
 And if you hit the break, it exits the innermost loop

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.880
 out to the place beyond the end of the loop.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:11.020
 So when this runs the first time and we say hello there,

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.520
 line is not done so it prints it.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.820
 So it prints out hello there and then goes up.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:17.860
 Then we type in again, we type finished.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.940
 And so it doesn't, it's not done so it prints it.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.380
 So now comes that print statement.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:25.580
 Then we type in done and now this becomes true.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:28.100
 And it comes out and runs the code

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:30.140
 beyond the end of the loop.

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:31.980
 The key is it doesn't go back.

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:35.620
 It's like once you've done a break, that loop is done.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:39.700
 So you look at basically the block that is the loop.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:42.140
 So here's kind of the loop block.

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:43.860
 And then the break goes to the line

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.640
 after the end of the loop block.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:52.620
 And you can think of this as sort of like

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:54.020
 just a hyperspace jump.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:56.780
 There is nothing really, this could be literally

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:59.580
 hundreds of lines with if statements.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.680
 And you could be running and doing all kinds of stuff

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.900
 and running and doing all these things.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.420
 And these things could run all kinds of ways, right?

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:08.900
 The point is is as soon as you hit a break statement,

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:10.420
 however much stuff is down here,

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:12.060
 however much stuff is up here,

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.720
 it exits to whatever the next line is

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.060
 beyond the end of the loop.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:22.220
 Continue is another loop control statement,

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:24.520
 but it works differently than break.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:27.420
 So break says get out of this loop.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:30.660
 Continue effectively says stop this iteration.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.360
 We're done with this iteration.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:36.140
 And so continue says go up back to the top of the loop.

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:37.520
 Oops, yeah.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.220
 Go up back to the top of the loop.

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:42.700
 And so here we read a line.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:45.340
 If the first character is a pound sign,

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:48.380
 line sub zero, if that first character's pound sign,

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:49.900
 we're gonna skip it.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:52.300
 And this is a way for us to make little comments

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:53.500
 in our typing.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.420
 And then if the line is done,

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:57.580
 we get out and otherwise we print it.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:59.340
 And so that's why there is no printout here

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.500
 because it comes in, runs, oops.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:10.140
 It comes in, this is true, and that goes back up.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:13.300
 But it comes back and prints out the next one

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:14.260
 and does another thing.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:15.820
 And so the loop continues,

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:18.420
 whereas the break ends the loop.

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:20.500
 And so again, the same kind of notion

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.860
 that you're sort of doing all kinds of complexity.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.780
 Wherever you're at in this loop,

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:28.340
 you hit continue and it doesn't go any further.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:30.860
 It goes back up and runs the question mark.

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:32.540
 It asks the question mark.

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:35.560
 And so, I mean, ask the question

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:37.580
 and it might exit the loop in that particular case.

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:38.960
 But this one here's a true,

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:41.260
 this is an infinite loop that I've constructed.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:42.660
 This is not an infinite loop

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:44.940
 because at some point the break gets us out of the loop.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.500
 And so it's an infinite loop with break to escape it.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:51.300
 And that's another common way to construct a loop.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:57.060
 So these loops that we've been drawing so far,

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:00.540
 the ones that use while as their keyword,

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:02.220
 are what are called indefinite loops.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:04.600
 And that's because they kind of go for a while

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:09.060
 till a break hits or until some value becomes true.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:12.180
 I mean, until as long as that value remains true.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:16.100
 So all the ones we've done so far are easy to look at

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:18.660
 and know that they look pretty good

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:20.460
 and they're probably gonna finish.

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:23.400
 But there are some times if they're long and complex

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.420
 and their exit or termination conditions

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:27.740
 are a little more complex,

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:30.220
 it's not clear that they're really gonna terminate.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:33.220
 And so we can use while loops for a lot of things,

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:36.320
 but for most of our looping,

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:38.260
 we're gonna use what are called indefinite loops.

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:40.420
 And that's what we're gonna talk about next.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:44.000
 (gentle music)

